WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:04.600
Film Courage: Story is the act of dreaming?

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:15.910
Adam Skelter: What the function of dreaming
is and what the function of story is and that’s

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.970
why I repeated it several times throughout
the series.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:28.140
And the process of dreaming is the way we
internalize information from the day into

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.679
our value system.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:31.899
So we’re all composed of a subconscious.

00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:35.300
The subconscious is constantly absorbing information.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.820
Now we have to learn to prioritize that information
or we won’t survive.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.450
We can’t even navigate the world around
us.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:45.420
It’s a kind of psychological equalibrium.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:54.650
So when we dream, we’re going through the
process of taking this information, connecting

00:00:54.650 --> 00:01:00.420
it to emotional values and weaving that info
our subconscious.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.770
And that right there is the fertile ground
metaphor.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.100
That’s where we’re planting our metaphors
and that’s where they grow from.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:13.399
So the dream is the render machine for our
lives.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:21.189
And so what we do when we’re telling stories
is we’re allowing ourselves to experience

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:22.500
certain things.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:29.210
We’re watching a series of events that have
emotional resonance with us which is why it

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.390
has such powerful effects on a culture.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:40.340
When you tell a story to a million people
that’s imposing, it’s offering up a new

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.859
way to look at the world.

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:47.850
And then if we internalize it emotionally,
if we sympathetically connect with it and

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:54.520
allow it into our subconscious and it effects
the our internal value systems, our sacred

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:01.189
and profane, then it completely changes the
way we see the world and that’s a really

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:03.020
powerful thing.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:10.830
So ultimately when we’re telling stories,
we’re allowing ourselves, we’re trying

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:16.310
to engage that subconscious part of each of
us and draw it into the conscious and change

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.640
the way we have our value systems.&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:28.910
Film Courage: Can storytellers change the
public consciousness through manufacturing

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:36.390
new stories or do you think it has to be in
the public’s space to be reenacted in film

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.820
and TV?

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:46.390
Can we manipulate…let’s suppose society
is on a downward spiral, whether it’s a

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:52.450
new breed of terrorism, whether it’s the
effects of people’s isolation through social

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:59.820
media and being online all the time, can we
infect…not infect because that has a negative

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:05.250
connotation, inject new stories that could
change things?

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.700
Or it has to follow what’s already out there
in the ether?

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.590
Adam: I’m a pretty positive person.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:17.510
I think like the truth of it is the world
is amazing right now.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.670
Politically speaking, yes it seems like an
awful place and people are behaving really

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.010
badly.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.530
But the truth of it is if you look at the
trends (are you familiar with Steven Pinker

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:28.470
‘Enlightenment Now’).

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:34.960
So read Pinker, read Chomsky, compare both
of them, compare and contrast, I don’t have

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.260
any loyalties either way but there’s really
good information in both of their writings.&nbsp;

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:46.510
Generally speaking we have a long way to go
before we are genuinely healthy, robust communities.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.960
That said, we’re doing really well.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:59.140
As storytellers, we have opportunities every
single day, even in Facebook posts or in writing

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:05.470
samples, or even in getting online and talking
about your situation, profoundly effects other

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.650
people emotionally.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:15.230
Honestly this is the time where we’re inundated
with endless amounts of stories.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:19.220
The image has never been more powerful…it’s
interesting because since the Internet and

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:24.900
I remember being asked by this one student
“Do you think illustration is dead?

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.810
Is art dead?”

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.470
Because it’s all online and it’s all about
animation.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.030
The image has never been more powerful.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:40.790
Think a hundred years ago, how difficult it
was to share just a printed image.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:44.780
Now&nbsp; you can be constantly producing stuff.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.710
When I was a kid, I dreamed of the day where
we could find out about all these artists

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:54.470
that I was pulling from or reading about all
these experiences that I had no access to.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:57.660
I’d have to go to the library or something
like that and I’d have to look through endless

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.740
amounts of books to find stuff.&nbsp;

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:06.520
Now go on Instagram or go on Facebook or look
at different artists and stuff.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.520
So I think the truth of it is, we’re in
a really good place.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.169
People are panicking because they are always
panicking.

00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:16.790
It always feels like the end of the world
because we’re living on the edge of eternity,

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:22.030
like we’re right at the edge looking over
having no idea what is going to happen tomorrow,

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.490
that’s just the human condition.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:30.560
But the reality is we’re doing really well
and when learning a lot and ultimately as

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.860
storytellers and writers, we’re doing everything
we can to generate new metaphors to help us

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:41.130
go into the dark night…God, that sounded
corny!

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:47.670
Film Courage:&nbsp; I see what you’re saying…and
not only that but the preservation of images…like

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:51.960
for me I wanted to look up an old shopping
mall that was in the San Francisco Bay Area

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.770
that I used to go to as a teenager and I think
it had been demolished…it was there [online]

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.120
as part of the [Forgotten] Malls of the America.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.760
And I didn’t realize there was an entire
Pinterest group/page dedicated to it and I

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.699
could relive these memories that would be
long lost…it brought out “Oh I remember

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:10.699
this spot.

00:06:10.699 --> 00:06:14.330
I remember going to this Sears.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:20.389
People had preserved those images, someone
had put them up on a blog, so that’s great

00:06:20.389 --> 00:06:34.400
because some of that would be lost had those
images not been preserved.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.240
How can we manipulate stories in a good way?

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:51.460
Not spin things in a bad way where I’ll
skip current news events but almost like THANK

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:55.110
YOU FOR SMOKING but in a positive manner?

00:06:55.110 --> 00:07:00.330
Adam: I mean that’s largely what rhetoric
is.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:08.130
Rhetoric is presenting information in a way
that persuades people to comply with you.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:16.090
But to me what really great art is and what
everybody decides what they want to do with

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:21.260
their art, do with their stories and I don’t
think people should be telling other people

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:25.000
what to write or what not to write, you get
to decide how you’re going to engage the

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.810
market and the market gets to decide whether
you are relevant or not.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:35.560
But ultimately the stories that are asking
important questions (challenging, important

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:46.690
questions) I believe bringing truth, challenging
existing beliefs, challenging values is one

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:48.400
of the most powerful things we can do

